q: shrun.c -- a shell that runs commands
spec: Grow `shtok.c` into a shell. Read standard input a line at a time,
spec: split each line into tokens by the same rules as `shtok` (the
spec: tokenizer is the same code), and run it. Print no prompt. A command's
spec: output is its own business: the shell itself prints nothing but
spec: error messages, and those go to standard error. Keep a status, the
spec: last command's, starting at 0; `$?` in a word now becomes that
spec: status as a decimal number.
spec:
spec: What a line does:
spec: - No tokens: nothing happens and the status is unchanged.
spec: - An operator token: print `shrun: operators not supported yet`,
spec:   status 2, run nothing.
spec: - A quote never closed: print `shrun: unterminated quote`, status 2.
spec: - `cd DIR` changes directory in the shell itself: status 0, or if
spec:   `chdir` fails print `shrun: cd: DIR: cannot cd` and status 1.
spec:   Without exactly one argument print `shrun: cd: usage: cd DIR`,
spec:   status 2.
spec: - `exit` ends the shell with the current status. `exit N`, N a whole
spec:   number from 0 up, ends it with N modulo 256. Any other argument
spec:   prints `shrun: exit: bad status`, status 2, and the shell goes on.
spec: - Anything else is a command: `fork`, then `execvp` with the words
spec:   as argv, and the parent waits. The status is the command's exit
spec:   code, or 128 plus the signal number if a signal ended it.
spec:
spec: If `execvp` fails the child prints `shrun: NAME: not found` (status
spec: 127) when the file does not exist, or `shrun: NAME: cannot execute`
spec: (status 126) for any other reason, NAME being the command word.
spec:
spec: At end of input the shell exits with the last status. Failed
spec: `fork`, `waitpid` or allocation print `shrun: fork failed`,
spec: `shrun: wait failed` or `shrun: out of memory` and give status 1.
file: shrun.c
