q: shpipe.c -- add pipelines
spec: Grow `shrun.c` so a line can be a pipeline: any number of commands,
spec: each joined to the next by the vertical bar (blanks around it or
spec: not). Everything from `shrun` still holds, with the prefix `shpipe:`
spec: in its messages (in vi, `:%s/shrun/shpipe/g` does it).
spec:
spec: A pipeline starts all its commands at once. Each command's standard
spec: output is a pipe feeding the next one's standard input; the first
spec: reads the shell's input and the last writes its output as usual.
spec: Close every spare end of every pipe, in the shell and in each child,
spec: or the last command never sees end of file. The shell waits for all
spec: of them, and the status of the pipeline is the last command's.
spec:
spec: Changes:
spec: - `cd` and `exit` are handled by the shell only when they are the
spec:   whole line. Inside a pipeline they are looked up like any command
spec:   (so they fail as `not found`).
spec: - A vertical bar with no command on one side (first on the line,
spec:   last on the line, or two in a row) prints `shpipe: empty command`,
spec:   status 2.
spec: - `<` and `>` are still not supported: print
spec:   `shpipe: redirection not supported yet`, status 2.
spec: - A pipe that cannot be made prints `shpipe: pipe failed`; a
spec:   failed fork prints `shpipe: fork failed`. Either way the commands
spec:   already started are still waited for, and the status is 1.
file: shpipe.c
