q: minish.c -- add redirection, finish the shell
spec: Grow `shpipe.c` into the last shell. Everything from `shpipe` still
spec: holds, with the prefix `minish:` in its messages. Add the two
spec: redirections: `< FILE` makes a command's standard input come from
spec: FILE, and `> FILE` makes its standard output go to FILE, which is
spec: created if missing and emptied if it exists. The file name is the
spec: word after the operator. They belong to whichever command of a
spec: pipeline they appear in, anywhere among its words, and win over the
spec: pipe: a command that redirects its output and is also followed by a
spec: bar leaves the next command with empty input. If one command gives
spec: the same redirection twice, the last one counts.
spec:
spec: Open the files in the child, after the pipe is wired up. If a file
spec: cannot be opened the child prints `minish: cannot open FILE` to
spec: standard error and exits 1, and the rest carries on as usual. A new
spec: file gets mode 0644.
spec:
spec: Changes to the errors, found scanning the line from the left, first
spec: problem wins, all with status 2:
spec: - An operator `<` or `>` with no word after it (the line ends, or
spec:   another operator follows) prints `minish: missing file name`.
spec: - A command left with no words (a line that is only `> f`, or a
spec:   pipeline stage that is only a redirection) prints
spec:   `minish: empty command`, like a bar with nothing beside it.
spec: - `cd` or `exit` with a redirection prints
spec:   `minish: builtins take no redirection`.
